1|Complete Blood Count with ESR (CBC + ESR)|cbc-esr-test|410|160|Haematology|Counts red cells, white cells and platelets and measures erythrocyte sedimentation rate.|Assessment of anaemia, infection, inflammation or general blood health.|No fasting is generally required.|Blood|An elevated ESR is nonspecific and does not identify the cause of inflammation.|CBC and ESR provide different information; neither alone confirms an infection.|CBC, ESR and peripheral smear are not interchangeable.
2|Blood Glucose (Fasting, PP or Random)|blood-sugar-test|70|20|Diabetes|Measures glucose in plasma at a specified collection time.|Diabetes screening or monitoring under clinical advice.|For fasting glucose, follow an 8-hour fast; PP samples need the prescribed after-meal interval, and random samples generally require no fast.|Blood|Fasting, post-meal and random results have different interpretation thresholds.|State which glucose sample you need when booking; these are alternative collection timings, not three tests for ₹20.|HbA1c reflects longer-term glycaemia, while plasma glucose reflects the sampled time.
3|Glycated Haemoglobin (HbA1c)|hba1c-test|720|260|Diabetes|Estimates average blood-glucose exposure over roughly the preceding 2–3 months.|Screening, diagnosis in suitable settings, or monitoring diabetes.|Fasting is not usually needed for HbA1c alone.|Blood|Anaemia, haemoglobin variants, recent transfusion and changes in red-cell turnover can affect interpretation.|HbA1c does not show immediate glucose changes or detect acute hypoglycaemia.|A clinician may also request fasting glucose or an oral glucose tolerance test.
4|Lipid Profile|lipid-profile-test|600|260|Heart health|Assesses cholesterol fractions and triglycerides to support cardiovascular risk assessment.|Cholesterol screening and cardiovascular risk evaluation.|Fasting requirements vary; a non-fasting sample is often acceptable, but fasting may be requested for high triglycerides or specific clinical needs.|Blood|Risk depends on age, blood pressure, diabetes, smoking and other factors—not cholesterol alone.|LDL calculated from a lipid profile can differ from a directly measured LDL test.|Discuss lipid results in the context of overall cardiovascular risk.
5|Liver Function Test (LFT)|liver-function-test|780|360|Liver health|Panel listed as ALT, AST, ALP, GGT, total/direct bilirubin, albumin and protein.|Investigation or monitoring of suspected liver or biliary problems.|Often no fasting is required; follow any clinician or laboratory instructions for the ordered panel.|Blood|Abnormal liver enzymes do not by themselves determine a specific diagnosis or liver function.|Confirm exact analytes included in the ₹360 panel before sample collection.|Interpret results alongside symptoms, medicines and any imaging.
6|Serum Creatinine|serum-creatinine-test|150|50|Kidney health|Measures blood creatinine, which is commonly used to estimate kidney filtration (eGFR).|Assessment of kidney function or monitoring medicines and chronic conditions.|Usually no fasting is necessary.|Blood|Muscle mass, diet and some medicines can affect creatinine; eGFR may require age and sex.|A single creatinine result cannot establish chronic kidney disease.|Urine albumin-creatinine ratio may add important kidney information.
7|Blood Urea / BUN|blood-urea-test|140|50|Kidney health|Measures urea nitrogen or urea in blood; reporting units vary by laboratory.|Kidney assessment or evaluation of hydration and protein metabolism.|Usually no fasting is needed unless combined with other fasting investigations.|Blood|Urea may rise with dehydration or dietary protein and is not a stand-alone kidney diagnosis.|BUN and urea values are related but differ numerically when reported in different units.|Creatinine and electrolytes can help give a fuller kidney assessment.
8|Urine Routine & Microscopy|urine-routine-microscopy-test|300|50|Urine and kidney|Checks urine chemistry and microscopic elements such as red/white cells, crystals and casts.|Symptoms of urinary disorders or evaluation of kidney or metabolic conditions.|No fasting is ordinarily necessary.|Urine|A clean-catch midstream specimen in a sterile container helps reduce contamination.|A routine urine test does not replace a urine culture when infection confirmation is required.|Menstrual contamination and delayed transport can alter urine findings.
9|Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH)|tsh-test|280|100|Thyroid|Measures TSH, a pituitary hormone used to assess thyroid regulation.|Initial evaluation or monitoring of thyroid dysfunction.|Fasting is generally not necessary.|Blood|TSH can be affected by pregnancy, medication and acute illness; free T4 may be needed.|If taking thyroid tablets, follow your clinician's advice on timing relative to collection.|TSH alone does not measure free thyroid-hormone concentrations.
10|Thyroid Function Test (Total T3, T4 & TSH)|thyroid-function-test-total|550|190|Thyroid|Measures total T3, total T4 and TSH as listed in the offer.|Further assessment of thyroid function where the clinician requests total hormones.|Fasting is generally not required.|Blood|Total thyroid hormone results are influenced by binding proteins and may differ from free-hormone results.|This ₹190 panel specifies total T3/T4; it is different from the free T3/free T4 panel.|Biotin supplements can interfere with some immunoassays—tell the laboratory.
11|Serum Electrolytes (Na, K, Cl)|serum-electrolytes-test|450|190|Kidney health|Measures sodium, potassium and chloride in blood.|Assessment of fluid balance, kidney conditions or relevant medicines.|Usually no fasting is needed.|Blood|Haemolysis during collection can falsely increase potassium; urgent abnormalities need medical review.|This panel does not necessarily include calcium, bicarbonate or magnesium.|Interpret electrolytes with renal function, symptoms and medicines.
12|ABO Blood Group & Rh Typing|blood-group-rh-test|150|90|Blood typing|Determines ABO blood group and RhD status.|Pregnancy, transfusion planning or when blood grouping is required.|No fasting is needed.|Blood|Blood group reports do not substitute for crossmatching before transfusion.|Weak or discrepant Rh typing needs established laboratory resolution procedures.|Use an authorised transfusion service for urgent transfusion decisions.
13|Vitamin D 25-Hydroxy|vitamin-d-test|1300|660|Vitamins|Measures serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D, the usual marker of vitamin D status.|Assessment of deficiency risk in appropriate clinical settings.|Fasting is not usually required.|Blood|Vitamin D level must be interpreted alongside symptoms, risk factors and sometimes calcium/PTH.|Routine testing is not necessary for everyone without a clinical indication.|Do not start high-dose supplements solely from online advice.
14|Vitamin B12 (Cobalamin)|vitamin-b12-test|1600|440|Vitamins|Measures vitamin B12 in serum.|Investigation of anaemia, neuropathy or nutritional and absorption concerns.|Fasting requirements vary by laboratory; ask before attending.|Blood|Recent supplementation can affect vitamin B12 levels; borderline results may need further evaluation.|A normal B12 level does not always exclude functional deficiency.|Folate and CBC are commonly reviewed alongside B12.
15|Serum Ferritin|ferritin-test|1320|460|Iron and anaemia|Measures ferritin, a marker of iron stores that can also rise with inflammation.|Evaluation of suspected iron deficiency or iron overload.|No fasting is usually necessary.|Blood|Ferritin may be high during inflammation even when iron availability is low.|Ferritin alone cannot explain every type of anaemia.|CBC and transferrin saturation may provide further context.
16|Iron Studies (Iron, TIBC, Saturation)|iron-studies-test|520|250|Iron and anaemia|Measures iron, iron-binding capacity and calculated transferrin saturation as listed.|Assessment of iron deficiency or iron overload with clinical context.|Morning collection or temporary fasting may be requested; confirm local method.|Blood|Serum iron fluctuates during the day and is altered by recent iron intake.|Confirm the listed components, including whether ferritin is separate.|Clinicians often compare iron studies with ferritin and CBC.
17|C-Reactive Protein (CRP), Quantitative|crp-test|550|250|Inflammation|Measures C-reactive protein, a nonspecific inflammatory marker.|Assessment of inflammation or response to treatment.|Fasting is typically not necessary.|Blood|Raised CRP does not establish bacterial infection or locate its source.|Standard CRP and high-sensitivity CRP are different tests for different clinical uses.|Interpret CRP along with symptoms, examination and other tests.
18|Serum Uric Acid|uric-acid-test|250|50|Kidney and metabolism|Measures urate in blood.|Evaluation of gout, kidney stones or relevant metabolic conditions.|Fasting is often not essential; follow clinician instructions.|Blood|Urate can be normal during an acute gout attack; high urate alone does not diagnose gout.|Diet, dehydration and some medicines may alter results.|Gout diagnosis depends on the full clinical picture.
19|Serum Calcium, Total|serum-calcium-test|200|60|Bone and minerals|Measures total blood calcium, including protein-bound and free calcium.|Investigation of bone, parathyroid or calcium-balance problems.|Fasting is generally not required.|Blood|Total calcium is influenced by albumin; ionised calcium may be requested in select situations.|Total calcium and ionised calcium are not the same measurement.|Interpret the result with albumin and clinical context.
20|Serum Phosphate|serum-phosphate-test|170|90|Bone and minerals|Measures inorganic phosphate in blood.|Assessment of renal or mineral/bone disorders.|Usually no fasting is needed unless requested.|Blood|Phosphate may fluctuate with meals, time of day and kidney function.|Phosphate is different from serum calcium and is regulated with PTH/vitamin D.|Clinical context determines whether retesting is needed.
21|Total Immunoglobulin E (IgE)|total-ige-test|1050|630|Allergy|Measures total IgE concentration in serum.|Selected allergy or immunology investigations.|No fasting is usually necessary.|Blood|High total IgE alone cannot diagnose an allergy to a specific substance.|Normal total IgE does not exclude allergic disease.|Specific-IgE tests or clinical allergy evaluation may be needed.
22|Hepatitis B Surface Antigen (HBsAg)|hbsag-test|1050|250|Infection screening|Detects hepatitis B surface antigen in blood.|Hepatitis B screening or work-up when clinically indicated.|No fasting is necessary.|Blood|A reactive screening result requires evaluation under the applicable testing algorithm.|HBsAg alone does not show immunity or fully stage hepatitis B infection.|Anti-HBs, anti-HBc and HBV DNA may be relevant depending on context.
23|Hepatitis C Antibody (Anti-HCV)|anti-hcv-test|1850|400|Infection screening|Detects antibodies to hepatitis C virus.|Hepatitis C exposure screening under clinical guidance.|No fasting is needed.|Blood|A positive antibody result does not by itself establish current active infection.|HCV RNA testing is usually used to determine current infection after a reactive antibody result.|A negative antibody test very early after exposure may need follow-up.
24|HIV 1/2 Antigen-Antibody, Fourth Generation|hiv-fourth-generation-test|1450|250|Infection screening|Screens for HIV-1/2 antibodies and p24 antigen using a fourth-generation assay.|HIV screening after possible exposure or for other indicated purposes.|No fasting is required.|Blood|Detection depends on time after exposure and the assay's window period.|Follow the applicable NACO testing and confirmation algorithm; no single reactive screen establishes a diagnosis.|Recent exposure may require further assessment or repeat testing.
25|Dengue NS1 Antigen & IgM/IgG Antibodies (ELISA)|dengue-ns1-igm-igg-test|1400|450|Fever and infection|Tests dengue NS1 antigen and dengue IgM/IgG antibodies as listed in the combination offer.|Investigation of suspected dengue based on symptoms and illness day.|No fasting is generally required.|Blood|Test performance depends on the day of illness; a negative result does not automatically rule out dengue.|Confirm the exact combination and methods before booking.|Warning signs such as bleeding, severe abdominal pain or lethargy need urgent evaluation.
26|Malaria Rapid Antigen (HRP2/pLDH)|malaria-antigen-test|400|200|Fever and infection|Uses rapid antigens to support detection of malaria parasites.|Investigation of fever when malaria is suspected.|No fasting is needed.|Blood|Negative rapid tests can miss some malaria cases, depending on parasite burden or species.|Blood-smear microscopy or repeat testing may be required under clinical advice.|Severe illness or suspected malaria needs prompt medical assessment.
27|Prothrombin Time / INR|pt-inr-test|450|190|Coagulation|Measures clotting time via the extrinsic pathway and reports INR where applicable.|Monitoring warfarin or evaluation of selected clotting disorders.|No fasting is normally required.|Blood|INR targets depend on the individual indication; do not adjust anticoagulants yourself.|Sample collection requires correct citrate-tube fill for reliable interpretation.|Tell the lab about anticoagulant medicines.
28|Activated Partial Thromboplastin Time (aPTT)|aptt-test|650|260|Coagulation|Measures clotting time through the intrinsic/common pathways.|Assessment of specific coagulation disorders or anticoagulant monitoring.|No fasting is normally needed.|Blood|aPTT may be affected by anticoagulants, sample handling and some factor deficiencies.|aPTT and PT/INR investigate different parts of coagulation.|Abnormal bleeding needs clinical review, not self-interpretation.
29|D-Dimer|d-dimer-test|1450|660|Coagulation|Measures fibrin breakdown products in blood.|Selected evaluation pathways for suspected thrombosis, together with clinical probability.|No fasting is usually required.|Blood|D-dimer may rise for many reasons; an elevated value alone does not confirm a clot.|Chest pain, sudden breathlessness or stroke-like symptoms require emergency care; do not wait for outpatient testing.|Use with clinician-assessed risk and appropriate imaging where required.
30|Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH)|ldh-test|450|120|Enzymes|Measures LDH, an enzyme found in many tissues.|Assessment of tissue injury or selected haematological conditions.|Fasting is usually not necessary.|Blood|Haemolysis during collection can falsely raise LDH.|An isolated high LDH does not identify the affected organ.|Other targeted tests are needed to determine the cause.
31|Serum Amylase|serum-amylase-test|510|160|Pancreas|Measures serum amylase enzyme activity.|Evaluation of suspected pancreatic or salivary-gland disease.|Fasting requirements vary; confirm before collection.|Blood|Amylase can rise for causes other than pancreatitis.|A normal amylase does not fully exclude pancreatitis depending on timing.|Lipase is often considered alongside or instead of amylase.
32|Serum Lipase|serum-lipase-test|600|250|Pancreas|Measures lipase enzyme activity.|Investigation of suspected acute pancreatitis when clinically indicated.|Fasting is not always required; follow local collection advice.|Blood|Raised lipase may occur in non-pancreatic conditions and needs clinical interpretation.|Severe upper abdominal pain, persistent vomiting or collapse requires urgent assessment.|Diagnosis of pancreatitis uses clinical findings and sometimes imaging.
33|Creatine Phosphokinase (CK/CPK), Total|cpk-total-test|470|260|Muscle|Measures total creatine kinase enzyme activity.|Assessment of muscle injury or selected neuromuscular conditions.|Fasting is usually unnecessary.|Blood|Strenuous exercise, injections and muscle injury can increase CK.|Total CK is not a reliable stand-alone test to diagnose a heart attack.|Discuss recent exercise and medicines such as statins with the clinician.
34|Rheumatoid Factor (RF)|rheumatoid-factor-test|680|200|Autoimmune|Measures rheumatoid factor antibodies.|Evaluation of inflammatory arthritis with history and examination.|No fasting is normally required.|Blood|Rheumatoid factor can be positive without rheumatoid arthritis, and negative in some affected patients.|Anti-CCP antibodies and inflammatory markers may be useful in context.|Do not diagnose rheumatoid arthritis from one test.
35|Anti-Streptolysin O (ASO) Titre|aso-titre-test|700|200|Infection and immunology|Measures antibodies against streptolysin O following group A streptococcal exposure.|Assessment of possible preceding streptococcal infection in relevant conditions.|No fasting is usually needed.|Blood|ASO titre rises after infection and is not an acute throat-swab substitute.|A single ASO value often needs clinical context and possibly paired titres.|Anti-DNase B may be considered in selected cases.
36|Serum Prolactin|prolactin-test|580|400|Hormones|Measures prolactin hormone in blood.|Work-up of irregular periods, galactorrhoea or selected pituitary concerns.|Fasting is not always essential; morning, rested collection may be preferred.|Blood|Stress, exercise, pregnancy and certain medications can raise prolactin.|Tell the lab about biotin supplements and prescribed medicines.|Mild elevations may warrant repeat sampling under advice.
37|Follicle-Stimulating Hormone (FSH)|fsh-test|600|400|Hormones|Measures pituitary follicle-stimulating hormone.|Assessment of fertility, menstrual, menopausal or selected gonadal conditions.|No fasting is generally required.|Blood|In menstruating patients, menstrual-cycle day affects FSH interpretation.|Record the cycle day and relevant medications when booking.|FSH is interpreted with clinical history and sometimes LH/oestradiol.
38|Luteinising Hormone (LH)|lh-test|600|400|Hormones|Measures pituitary luteinising hormone.|Investigation of menstrual, ovulatory or gonadal conditions.|No fasting is generally necessary.|Blood|LH rises sharply around ovulation; a single result depends on cycle timing.|Cycle day, sex and hormonal medicines affect interpretation.|LH is often assessed with FSH and other hormones.
39|Total Testosterone|total-testosterone-test|900|460|Hormones|Measures total testosterone including protein-bound hormone.|Assessment of androgen-related symptoms under clinical guidance.|Fasting or early-morning collection may be recommended depending on clinical question and laboratory protocol.|Blood|Testosterone varies by time of day; a low value generally needs appropriate repeat confirmation.|Total testosterone is not the same as free testosterone.|Interpret results with sex, age, symptoms and SHBG where needed.
40|Beta hCG (Quantitative)|beta-hcg-test|850|460|Pregnancy and hormones|Measures beta human chorionic gonadotropin in blood.|Early pregnancy evaluation or monitoring in selected clinical situations.|No fasting is needed.|Blood|Serial beta hCG changes may matter more than one isolated measurement.|A single hCG value cannot determine pregnancy location or viability.|Pain or bleeding in pregnancy requires timely medical care.
41|Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA)|cea-test|1050|600|Tumour markers|Measures CEA, a tumour-associated marker used mainly in specified monitoring contexts.|Follow-up in selected cancers when requested by an oncologist.|No fasting is generally required.|Blood|CEA is not suitable as a stand-alone general cancer-screening test.|Smoking and benign conditions can influence CEA.|Cancer diagnosis requires appropriate clinical assessment and other investigations.
42|Cancer Antigen 125 (CA-125)|ca-125-test|1210|600|Tumour markers|Measures CA-125 protein in blood.|Monitoring or investigating selected gynaecological conditions when clinically indicated.|No fasting is usually necessary.|Blood|CA-125 can rise in benign conditions such as menstruation or endometriosis.|CA-125 alone cannot screen for or diagnose ovarian cancer reliably.|Ultrasound and clinical assessment may be required.
43|Urine Albumin-Creatinine Ratio (ACR)|urine-acr-test|680|360|Kidney health|Measures urinary albumin relative to creatinine to assess albumin leakage.|Screening/monitoring kidney disease especially with diabetes or hypertension.|No fasting is generally needed; early-morning urine is often preferred.|Urine|Fever, strenuous exercise or urinary infection may transiently elevate urine ACR.|Repeat confirmation may be advised for an abnormal result.|eGFR and urine ACR provide complementary kidney-risk information.
44|Prostate-Specific Antigen (PSA), Total|total-psa-test|900|400|Men's health|Measures total PSA in blood.|Selected assessment of prostate conditions after shared decision-making.|No fasting is generally required.|Blood|PSA may rise from benign enlargement, inflammation or recent instrumentation.|An elevated PSA does not by itself diagnose prostate cancer.|Discuss timing after infection or instrumentation with the clinician.
45|Folic Acid (Serum Folate)|serum-folate-test|1350|560|Vitamins|Measures folate concentration in serum.|Evaluation of possible folate deficiency or selected anaemia.|Fasting may be requested; follow the laboratory's method-specific instructions.|Blood|Recent folic-acid supplements or meals can influence serum folate.|Folate deficiency and B12 deficiency can have overlapping blood findings.|Assess B12 status as clinically indicated before high-dose folate.
46|VDRL Test|vdrl-test|450|220|Infection screening|Performs a non-treponemal serological test used in syphilis evaluation.|Syphilis screening or follow-up as directed by a clinician.|No fasting is needed.|Blood|Reactive VDRL generally requires evaluation with an appropriate treponemal test.|False-positive reactions can occur; titres and clinical context matter.|Use the appropriate algorithm for confirmation and follow-up.
47|TyphiDot IgM/IgG|typhidot-test|750|550|Fever and infection|Detects IgM/IgG antibodies related to Salmonella Typhi.|Limited adjunctive assessment of suspected enteric fever.|No fasting is necessary.|Blood|TyphiDot antibody testing has limitations and should not replace clinical evaluation or blood culture.|Blood culture, especially before antibiotics, is preferred for confirmatory diagnosis when feasible.|Do not delay evaluation of persistent fever.
48|Thyroid Function Panel (Free T3, Free T4, TSH)|thyroid-function-test-free|850|330|Thyroid|Measures free T3, free T4 and TSH.|Assessment of thyroid function when free hormones are clinically required.|Fasting is generally not needed.|Blood|Biotin and certain drugs can interfere with some thyroid immunoassays.|This ₹330 panel measures free T3/free T4, unlike the ₹190 total T3/T4 panel.|Pregnancy and acute illness may change thyroid test interpretation.
49|Platelet Count|platelet-count-test|150|100|Haematology|Counts platelets in blood.|Evaluation of bleeding risk or monitoring low/high platelet counts.|No fasting is generally required.|Blood|Platelet clumping can produce a falsely low automated count.|Bleeding symptoms or very low results require prompt clinical review.|A blood smear may be needed to verify unexpected results.
50|Haemoglobin (Hb)|haemoglobin-test|150|60|Haematology|Measures haemoglobin concentration in blood.|Assessment of anaemia or monitoring relevant treatment.|No fasting is generally required.|Blood|Low haemoglobin indicates anaemia but not its cause.|Interpret with age, sex, pregnancy and CBC indices where relevant.|Ferritin, B12 or other tests may be needed based on clinical context.
51|Complete Haemogram (CBC, ESR & Peripheral Smear)|complete-haemogram-ps-test|500|230|Haematology|Combines CBC, ESR and peripheral-smear examination as advertised.|Assessment of anaemia, blood-cell abnormalities or inflammation.|Fasting is usually not needed.|Blood|Peripheral smear findings require clinical interpretation and are not a diagnosis by themselves.|Confirm the smear review scope before booking the ₹230 package.|CBC and smear results may warrant further directed tests.
52|Absolute Eosinophil Count (AEC)|aec-test|220|100|Haematology|Measures absolute eosinophil concentration in blood.|Evaluation of eosinophilia with possible allergic, drug or parasitic causes.|No fasting is generally required.|Blood|Elevated eosinophils do not identify a specific allergy or infection.|AEC should be interpreted with CBC and symptoms.|Other testing depends on the clinical history.
53|Bilirubin, Total & Direct|bilirubin-total-direct-test|140|120|Liver health|Measures total and direct bilirubin; indirect may be calculated from the difference.|Investigation of jaundice or selected liver/biliary conditions.|Fasting is often not essential, although the lab may advise preparation.|Blood|Bilirubin interpretation depends on direct versus indirect predominance.|Bilirubin by itself cannot determine the cause of jaundice.|Liver enzymes and clinical evaluation may be needed.
54|Serum Magnesium|serum-magnesium-test|450|200|Bone and minerals|Measures magnesium concentration in blood.|Assessment of electrolyte disturbances or selected neuromuscular symptoms.|No fasting is usually required.|Blood|Serum magnesium may not perfectly reflect total-body stores.|Haemolysis can interfere with some magnesium measurements.|Potassium and calcium may be checked as clinically indicated.
55|Serum Iron|serum-iron-test|550|160|Iron and anaemia|Measures circulating serum iron concentration.|Part of evaluation of suspected abnormal iron status.|Collection time and fasting may matter; check with the laboratory.|Blood|Serum iron varies during the day and does not assess iron stores by itself.|Ferritin and transferrin saturation provide complementary information.|Avoid interpreting isolated serum iron as a diagnosis of iron deficiency.
56|Alanine Aminotransferase (ALT / SGPT)|alt-sgpt-test|150|60|Liver health|Measures ALT enzyme activity in blood.|Assessment of liver-cell injury or monitoring medicines.|No fasting is usually necessary.|Blood|ALT may rise with multiple liver and non-liver conditions.|ALT alone does not measure overall liver function or disease severity.|AST, bilirubin and other tests may be considered.
57|Aspartate Aminotransferase (AST / SGOT)|ast-sgot-test|150|60|Liver health|Measures AST enzyme activity in blood.|Assessment of possible liver or muscle injury.|No fasting is usually needed.|Blood|AST is present in muscle and other tissues, so it is not liver-specific.|Recent strenuous exercise may affect AST.|ALT and other markers aid interpretation.
58|Anti-Thyroid Peroxidase Antibodies (Anti-TPO)|anti-tpo-test|1700|560|Thyroid|Measures antibodies against thyroid peroxidase.|Assessment of suspected autoimmune thyroid disease.|No fasting is typically needed.|Blood|Positive anti-TPO antibodies support autoimmune thyroid disease but do not alone measure thyroid function.|TSH and free T4 show hormone status, whereas anti-TPO is an antibody test.|Treatment decisions depend on thyroid function and clinical context.
59|Potassium (Serum / Plasma)|potassium-test|190|80|Kidney health|Measures potassium electrolyte concentration.|Assessment of electrolyte disorders and relevant treatments.|No fasting is generally necessary.|Blood|Collection-related haemolysis can falsely elevate potassium.|Severe potassium imbalance can be dangerous; urgent review may be needed.|Interpret with kidney function, medicines and ECG when indicated.
60|Renal Profile (Urea, Creatinine, Uric Acid, Electrolytes)|renal-profile-test|1150|320|Kidney health|Panel advertised as urea, creatinine, uric acid and electrolytes.|Basic evaluation of kidney-related measurements.|Fasting is usually not needed unless ordered with other fasting tests.|Blood|Normal blood tests do not necessarily exclude early kidney damage.|Confirm which electrolytes are in the ₹320 renal profile before booking.|Urine ACR and calculated eGFR may add important information.
61|LDL Cholesterol, Direct|direct-ldl-test|310|120|Heart health|Directly measures low-density lipoprotein cholesterol.|Assessment of lipid-associated cardiovascular risk where direct LDL is indicated.|Fasting often is not required, depending on the method and clinical request.|Blood|Direct LDL differs from calculated LDL reported in many lipid panels.|LDL treatment targets depend on individual cardiovascular risk.|A full lipid profile and risk assessment may still be needed.
62|Troponin I, Quantitative|troponin-i-test|1450|760|Cardiac markers|Measures cardiac troponin I concentration.|Clinician-led evaluation of suspected heart-muscle injury.|Fasting is not required.|Blood|Troponin elevations indicate myocardial injury, not necessarily a heart attack; serial testing and ECG may be required.|If chest pain, sweating or severe breathlessness is present, go to emergency care instead of booking routine home collection.|Troponin is time-sensitive and needs urgent clinical interpretation.
63|Serum Cortisol (AM / PM)|cortisol-test|1100|500|Hormones|Measures cortisol at a specified morning or evening collection time.|Assessment of selected adrenal or endocrine conditions.|Fasting needs depend on the requested endocrine protocol.|Blood|Cortisol has a daily rhythm, so collection time is essential for interpretation.|Specify AM or PM and record relevant steroid medicines, without stopping them unless advised.|An isolated cortisol value may not be diagnostic.
64|Gamma-Glutamyl Transferase (GGT)|ggt-test|250|200|Liver health|Measures GGT enzyme activity in blood.|Assessment of hepatobiliary abnormalities or follow-up.|No fasting is usually required unless combined tests specify it.|Blood|GGT is sensitive but nonspecific and may rise with medications or alcohol exposure.|GGT alone cannot establish a diagnosis of liver disease.|Interpret alongside ALP, ALT, AST and clinical history.
65|Serum Total Protein|total-protein-test|190|50|Liver and nutrition|Measures total serum protein, mainly albumin and globulins.|Assessment of selected liver, kidney or nutritional conditions.|No fasting is generally necessary.|Blood|An abnormal total protein does not identify which protein fraction is altered.|Albumin/globulin values or electrophoresis may be requested when indicated.|Hydration can influence concentration measurements.
66|Ionised Calcium|ionised-calcium-test|685|260|Bone and minerals|Measures physiologically active unbound calcium in blood.|Assessment of calcium imbalance in relevant settings.|Fasting is usually not required; follow specimen-handling instructions.|Blood|Ionised calcium is sensitive to specimen pH and collection/handling delays.|Ionised calcium differs from total calcium, especially with abnormal albumin or acid-base status.|Prompt specimen transport is important for reliable measurement.
